# 1. LRU Cache — C#

### Requirements

- `Get(key)` → return value and mark it recently used.
- `Put(key, value)` → insert/update.
- When capacity is exceeded, remove **least recently used** item.
- `Get` and `Put` should be **O(1)**.

### Design

Use:

```
Dictionary<TKey, Node>
              +
      Doubly Linked List

Head → Most Recently Used
Tail → Least Recently Used
```

### Why both?

`Dictionary` gives O(1) lookup.

Doubly linked list gives O(1) removal and insertion.

```
HEAD
 ↓
[A] ↔ [B] ↔ [C] ↔ [D]
                    ↑
                   TAIL

A = recently used
D = least recently used
```

### C# Implementation

```csharp
public class LruCache<TKey, TValue>
{
    private class Node
    {
        public TKey Key;
        public TValue Value;
        public Node Prev;
        public Node Next;

        public Node(TKey key, TValue value)
        {
            Key = key;
            Value = value;
        }
    }

    private readonly int _capacity;
    private readonly Dictionary<TKey, Node> _map;

    private readonly Node _head;
    private readonly Node _tail;

    private readonly object _lock = new();

    public LruCache(int capacity)
    {
        if (capacity <= 0)
            throw new ArgumentException("Capacity must be positive");

        _capacity = capacity;
        _map = new Dictionary<TKey, Node>();

        _head = new Node(default, default);
        _tail = new Node(default, default);

        _head.Next = _tail;
        _tail.Prev = _head;
    }

    public TValue Get(TKey key)
    {
        lock (_lock)
        {
            if (!_map.TryGetValue(key, out var node))
                return default;

            MoveToFront(node);

            return node.Value;
        }
    }

    public void Put(TKey key, TValue value)
    {
        lock (_lock)
        {
            if (_map.TryGetValue(key, out var existing))
            {
                existing.Value = value;
                MoveToFront(existing);
                return;
            }

            var node = new Node(key, value);

            _map[key] = node;
            AddToFront(node);

            if (_map.Count > _capacity)
            {
                var lru = _tail.Prev;

                Remove(lru);
                _map.Remove(lru.Key);
            }
        }
    }

    private void MoveToFront(Node node)
    {
        Remove(node);
        AddToFront(node);
    }

    private void AddToFront(Node node)
    {
        node.Next = _head.Next;
        node.Prev = _head;

        _head.Next.Prev = node;
        _head.Next = node;
    }

    private void Remove(Node node)
    {
        node.Prev.Next = node.Next;
        node.Next.Prev = node.Prev;
    }
}
```

### Interview questions

**Why not just use Dictionary?**

Dictionary doesn't maintain ordering.

**Why doubly linked list instead of singly?**

Because removing an arbitrary node requires O(1). With a singly linked list, we'd need to find its previous node.

**Why lock?**

Because concurrent `Get` and `Put` modify both the dictionary and linked list.

