## Parking Spot

```csharp
public enum SpotType
{
    Motorcycle,
    Compact,
    Large
}

public abstract class ParkingSpot
{
    public string Id { get; }
    public SpotType Type { get; }

    protected Vehicle Vehicle { get; private set; }

    protected ParkingSpot(string id, SpotType type)
    {
        Id = id;
        Type = type;
    }

    public bool IsFree => Vehicle == null;

    public virtual bool CanFit(Vehicle vehicle)
    {
        return false;
    }

    public void Park(Vehicle vehicle)
    {
        if (!IsFree)
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Spot occupied");

        if (!CanFit(vehicle))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Vehicle cannot fit");

        Vehicle = vehicle;
    }

    public void Vacate()
    {
        Vehicle = null;
    }
}
```

---

## Strategy for Spot Allocation

Don't put allocation logic directly inside `ParkingLot`.

```csharp
public interface IParkingSpotStrategy
{
    ParkingSpot FindSpot(
        IEnumerable<ParkingFloor> floors,
        Vehicle vehicle);
}
```

Example:

```csharp
public class NearestSpotStrategy : IParkingSpotStrategy
{
    public ParkingSpot FindSpot(
        IEnumerable<ParkingFloor> floors,
        Vehicle vehicle)
    {
        foreach (var floor in floors)
        {
            foreach (var spot in floor.Spots)
            {
                if (spot.IsFree && spot.CanFit(vehicle))
                    return spot;
            }
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```

Now tomorrow the requirement changes:

> Allocate the spot closest to elevator.
> 

We can add:

```
NearestSpotStrategy
ElevatorProximityStrategy
HandicapPriorityStrategy
EVChargingStrategy
```

without changing `ParkingLot`.

That's a strong **Open/Closed Principle** example.

