# 4. Elevator System — C#

This is usually the most interesting of the four.

### Requirements

- Multiple elevators
- Floors
- External requests: UP/DOWN
- Internal requests: destination floor
- Elevator movement
- Door opening/closing
- Elevator assignment
- Scheduling strategy

---

## Architecture

```
                ElevatorController
                       |
          +------------+------------+
          |            |            |
      Elevator 1   Elevator 2   Elevator 3
          |
       ElevatorState
          |
    IDLE / MOVING / DOOR_OPEN
```

---

# Request

```csharp
public enum Direction
{
    Up,
    Down,
    None
}

public class ElevatorRequest
{
    public int Floor { get; }
    public Direction Direction { get; }

    public ElevatorRequest(int floor, Direction direction)
    {
        Floor = floor;
        Direction = direction;
    }
}
```

---

# Elevator

```csharp
public class Elevator
{
    public int Id { get; }

    public int CurrentFloor { get; private set; }

    public Direction Direction { get; private set; }

    private readonly SortedSet<int> _upRequests = new();
    private readonly SortedSet<int> _downRequests =
        new(Comparer<int>.Create((a, b) => b.CompareTo(a)));

    public Elevator(int id)
    {
        Id = id;
        CurrentFloor = 0;
        Direction = Direction.None;
    }

    public void AddRequest(int floor)
    {
        if (floor > CurrentFloor)
            _upRequests.Add(floor);
        else if (floor < CurrentFloor)
            _downRequests.Add(floor);
    }
}
```

---

# Why SortedSet?

Suppose elevator is at floor 5 and requests are:

```
2
8
10
6
```

When moving UP:

```
6 → 8 → 10
```

After finishing upward requests, it can process downward requests:

```
2
```

This resembles the **SCAN / elevator scheduling algorithm**.

---

# Elevator Selection Strategy

```csharp
public interface IElevatorSelectionStrategy
{
    Elevator Select(
        IEnumerable<Elevator> elevators,
        ElevatorRequest request);
}
```

Simple implementation:

```csharp
public class NearestElevatorStrategy
    : IElevatorSelectionStrategy
{
    public Elevator Select(
        IEnumerable<Elevator> elevators,
        ElevatorRequest request)
    {
        return elevators
            .OrderBy(e =>
                Math.Abs(e.CurrentFloor - request.Floor))
            .First();
    }
}
```

Again, this allows replacing the algorithm:

```
NearestElevatorStrategy
        ↓
DirectionAwareStrategy
        ↓
LoadAwareStrategy
        ↓
ETA based strategy
```

without modifying `ElevatorController`.

---

# Elevator Controller

```csharp
public class ElevatorController
{
    private readonly List<Elevator> _elevators;

    private readonly IElevatorSelectionStrategy _strategy;

    public ElevatorController(
        List<Elevator> elevators,
        IElevatorSelectionStrategy strategy)
    {
        _elevators = elevators;
        _strategy = strategy;
    }

    public void RequestElevator(
        int floor,
        Direction direction)
    {
        var request = new ElevatorRequest(floor, direction);

        var elevator =
            _strategy.Select(_elevators, request);

        elevator.AddRequest(floor);
    }
}
```

